WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.635
-You know our first guest
from his work on

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.903
"Saturday Night Live."

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.171
He co-wrote and stars in
"The King of Staten Island,"

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.673
which is available
on demand now.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:10.343
Here's my conversation with our
friend Pete Davidson.

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:12.478
Pete, buddy, it is
so nice to see you.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:13.612
How are you doing?

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:15.014
-I'm good, man.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:18.518
You know, just chillin' in
my mom's basement, being safe.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:21.454
-That is -- The rumor was that
you were in your mom's basement.

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:23.856
I was on "Howard Stern"
this morning,

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.092
and he was saying that he
talked to you last week

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:29.461
and was busting you on the fact
that you have a movie

00:00:29.461 --> 00:00:32.164
that you wrote and you starred
in and it's coming out

00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:34.567
and there's no premiere party
and there's no red carpet

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.736
and you are, in fact,
in your mom's basement.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.639
-Yep. Couldn't have
turned out better for me.

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:41.907
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:41.907 --> 00:00:43.877
-There is something really
fitting on making

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:47.380
a movie that is -- I mean,
I think there's

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.816
been very few films that are
a love letter to Staten Island,

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.851
which I do believe this movie is
in a lot of ways.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.553
And, yet, it seems like the
price you paid for that is --

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:57.156
you have to be in Staten Island
the week it opened.

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.859
Like, it did not allow you any
escape from Staten Island.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.494
There is something
to the fact that this is,

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:05.264
I mean, a film about
Staten Island,

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:07.566
real people from Staten Island,
people who like being there,

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.102
who feel it's undervalued.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:10.436
What have your friends
thought of the film?

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.671
Because, obviously,
you take a lot on your back

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:14.473
when you make a film
about your hometown.

00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:16.709
-My friends really dug it.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.478
The main comment I got
from my friends --

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.682
they would, like, pause
the movie and they would call me

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:26.619
and they'd be like, "Bro, I know
Marisa Tomei is your mom.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.757
And don't take any offense to
this, but, bro, she's hot."

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:34.226
And I'm like, "There's
no offense to that at all.

00:01:34.226 --> 00:01:37.697
It's a fictional -- Yeah."

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.899
So that's the
biggest comment I got.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.436
-How does your mom feel about
the casting of Marisa Tomei?

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:46.673
-Oh, my mom's just thrilled.
We're all just such big fans.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.841
And she's so incredible
and such a genius.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:51.744
So, like, feel like
we got away with something.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.913
Like, we're so thrilled
to have her in it.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:57.317
-She's amazing.
Your grandfather is in the film.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:00.086
-Yeah, my actual grandfather's
in the film.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:03.055
He came to set every day
and hung out.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:05.925
And he told me that he could
tell that I was doing well

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.127
because of the food
that they were serving.

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.229
He was like, "Peter,
there's pork chops today,

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.099
so I know everything's
going alright."

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.402
-Was it the plan from
the beginning to have him

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:20.072
in that graduation scene
or did he sort of talk his way

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:21.341
on to the set?

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:23.710
-I think Judd just pulled me
aside and was like,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.545
"I think he would crush it."

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.546
And then I was like,
"Yeah, he probably would.

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.915
And then he arguably has
my favorite scene

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:31.217
in the entire movie.

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.418
-It's really funny.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.821
Don't take this the wrong way,
but when he first

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.123
opened his mouth,
I had this thought --

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:40.659
"Oh, Pete knows that person."

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:42.929
It did not seem like
someone you found

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.897
via a normal casting table.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.267
-Yeah. He really knocked it
out of the park.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.935
We're really happy for him.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:52.605
-So, you know, this movie is,
you know, based, obviously,

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.608
on your real life,
and, you know, the crux

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.610
of it is that you lost
your father,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.245
who was a fireman, in 9/11.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.513
I have heard you talk about that
onstage before,

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:05.685
but I have to imagine writing
about it for a film --

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.119
is it cathartic?

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.121
Is it painful?

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:09.722
Like, what was
that process like?

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.824
-It was a little bit of both.

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:14.761
I think it could've been a lot
worse if Judd and the crew

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.629
wasn't so sensitive
to my feelings.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:19.231
They really made me
feel, like, comfortable.

00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:22.734
So it was definitely more of
a cathartic experience,

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:26.438
and it definitely made me feel
a lot better as a person.

00:03:26.438 --> 00:03:31.244
-Judd is, obviously, well-known
and deservedly receives

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:34.413
a lot of praise for helping
people tell their stories,

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:37.016
but before Judd got on board,
like, earlier drafts,

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.251
when it was just --
And who's the friend of yours

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.319
that you co-wrote it with?

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.389
-Oh, Dave Sirus,
the great Dave Sirus.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:46.492
-So, you guys --
Early drafts were less about

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.494
this cathartic journey
and more about --

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:49.629
Well, why don't
you tell everybody

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.029
what the early drafts were like.

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:53.999
-Yeah. Originally,
it was just me and my friend

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:56.302
were writing this movie about
getting my mom laid,

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.272
and it just got
overly disgusting.

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:02.107
And then Judd turned it
and weaved it into this

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:05.344
120 pages of emotion
and an actual story,

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:06.778
so thank God for him.

00:04:06.778 --> 00:04:09.449
-A far different film.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.684
-A very different film for sure.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.653
-You have some --
I mean, some classic,

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:16.089
well-known, great actors.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:20.159
Like, we mentioned Marisa Tomei.
Steve Buscemi is outstanding.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.228
-Oh, yeah.
-Bill Burr is fantastic.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:25.964
One of my favorite stand-ups,
and he is just a great actor.

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:29.168
Is this someone you had always
wanted to play that part?

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.370
-Bill Burr is a hero of mine,

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.706
And I think he's the greatest
stand-up comic ever.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.075
So, like,
Judd and I were just like,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.677
"If there's any way
we can get him to do --"

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:38.844
He was another guy we were like,

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:40.813
"I hope we can
get him to do this,

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.114
you know, 'cause he's so great."

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:45.017
And it was a dream come true
getting to work with him.

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:46.085
He destroyed it.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.419
-He was wonderful.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.889
And it's really fun
to watch, you know,

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:53.759
someone you know as a stand-up,
like, just hold their own,

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.062
you know, in scenes with someone
like Marisa Tomei.

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:58.030
And I should also note,
I felt the same way about you.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.833
Like, you and Bill are two
people I know sort of uniquely

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.236
from watching you
be versions of yourself.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:04.470
It was really fun to watch
this movie

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.139
and sort of forget you
were Pete.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:08.440
It was great.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.710
I mean, did you feel --
Is the burden of acting --

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.613
And I know you've done movies,
but is the burden of acting

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:16.348
when you are playing a version
of yourself bigger or is it

00:05:16.348 --> 00:05:18.050
do you feel more comfortable?

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.153
-Oh, I felt a lot
more comfortable,

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:23.789
because it's just like,
"If nobody likes this,

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:25.224
it's just they don't like me."

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:29.762
It's not like
I was reaching or anything.

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.563
It's just people
just don't like me.

00:05:31.563 --> 00:05:34.333
That was my way out of it.

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.435
-You said your Staten Island
friends have been pleased with

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:38.437
the way that the film
turned out.

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:40.973
Were people excited when
there was a film set

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.808
on Staten Island
in the beginning?

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:47.346
-People from Staten Island
would -- It's very --

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:48.981
A lot of people
were excited about it,

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.216
but a lot of people were,
like, very frustrated

00:05:51.216 --> 00:05:53.753
with the traffic
that it brought.

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:54.887
-I see.

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.622
-Like, you know how,
normally, like,

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:58.924
if anybody's shooting anything
and people will

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:00.292
stop by and be like,
"What is this?

00:06:00.292 --> 00:06:02.094
Oh, my God. This is amazing."

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.396
A lot of people would stop by
and be like,

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:05.731
"Hey, you're blocking
my driveway."

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:09.101
-You know what?

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:10.703
I can't believe
it took this long,

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.137
considering the show
is based in New York,

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:15.240
but I think it's safe to say
this is the first era

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.544
in "SNL" where there are
two actors, two cast members,

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.046
from Staten Island,
you and Colin Jost.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:21.414
-Yeah.

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:25.717
-Colin also did a movie called
"Staten Island Summer."

00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:29.088
Do you think he regrets
now going first

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:32.458
and not naming his movie
"The King of Staten Island"?

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:35.227
-Probably. I don't know.

00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:38.597
Colin's crushing it in Montauk
with the waves, man.

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:42.101
Colin doesn't care about
the Internet fodder.

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:43.936
Colin's destroying the world.

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.839
-He's "The Monarch of Montauk."
That's what you're saying.

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:50.576
-Yeah. He's the man.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.444
-Alright, we're going to
come back with more

00:06:52.444 --> 00:06:53.579
Pete Davidson right after this.

